\begin{abstract}
Atrial fibrillation (AF) raises stroke risk, and electrocardiogram (ECG) models that predict its onset are explained only after training, by attribution methods that agree poorly. We asked whether a model whose ECG evidence passes through readable factors predicts incident AF from one sinus-rhythm 12-lead ECG more accurately than a clinical model and a convolutional neural network (CNN), and where in the cardiac cycle its evidence lies. We propose the block-term tensor network (BTTN), a Cox survival network on a frequency by time by lead spectrogram tensor. Each block projects the tensor onto a frequency factor and a lead factor, and age, sex and ECG intervals enter a separate linear clinical term that bypasses these factors. Incident AF was defined from coded hospital diagnoses in MIMIC-IV-ECG. At one year, BTTN with the clinical term had the highest area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) of the methods compared, 0.7776 (95\% confidence interval [0.7664, 0.7888]), against 0.7597 [0.7488, 0.7713] for the clinical model and 0.7350 [0.7226, 0.7469] for the CNN. The ECG term alone reached 0.7397 [0.7275, 0.7517], comparable to the CNN. One survival model gave AUROCs of 0.723 to 0.745 at horizons from 120 days to 5 years after the ECG, with a 30-day blanking window. In the survival sample, which over-represents patients with AF, observed one-year AF rose from 0.55\% in the lowest risk decile to 11.4\% in the highest. Per unit of signal energy, beat-aligned occlusion changed the ECG term's risk most in the PR window. External validation outside this single health system is the next requirement.
\end{abstract}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig-beat-saliency.png}
    \caption{Response of the ECG-only BTTN to beat-aligned occlusion, as the mean over 120 ECGs. The model was trained on the sample of the survival design. The ECGs are those of patients with incident AF to which the model gave the highest risk, passing over ECGs that could not be read or had fewer than three detected beats. Each panel shows one lead. The black trace is the mean of the ECGs' median beats, with each lead scaled to a maximum absolute amplitude of 1; the vertical scale is not shown. Time is relative to the R peak detected in lead II (dotted line). Occlusion windows of 12 samples (24~ms) are centred every 25~ms from 350~ms before to 425~ms after the R peak, with each centre rounded to a whole sample towards the R peak. Each shaded band spans the 25~ms step around its nominal centre. For each window, the window was set to zero in every beat of one lead, the spectrogram was rebuilt and the drop in predicted risk was recorded. Shading depth shows the mean drop, with a negative mean set to zero, divided by the mean signal energy removed plus one tenth of that energy averaged over all leads and windows. Depth is relative to the largest value in the figure, and windows below 3 percent of it are not shaded. The P, PR, QRS, ST and T windows of the text group the windows centred 225 to 150~ms before the R peak, 125 to 75~ms before, 50~ms before to 25~ms after, 50 to 125~ms after, and 150 to 375~ms after. The windows centred 350 to 250~ms before and at 400 and 425~ms after the R peak belong to none of the five. Table~\ref{tab:occlusion} gives the span of the beat that each group occludes. The shading describes the response of the fitted model to occlusion and is not an effect on the risk of AF.}
    \label{fig:beat-saliency}
\end{figure}

